WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:06.720
What does 87 cents have to do with a&nbsp;
multimillion euro election campaign?&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.840
And how come this neo-Nazi party is&nbsp;
getting funding from the German state?!&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.800
It's all to do with the way political&nbsp;
parties are financed in Germany.&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.840
The general principles of this party&nbsp;
regulation and financial regulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:38.320
I think they are fair, or at least they are&nbsp;
well intended. But we have seen in the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.840
that there have been many loopholes and&nbsp;
that the system could be manipulated.&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:50.320
In Germany, there's no limit on how much&nbsp;
parties can spend on election campaigning.&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.080
But some parties have more to spend than others.&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.200
Here is how the disparity looked at&nbsp;
the last federal election in 2017.&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.680
You can clearly see – the two biggest&nbsp;
parties, the Christian Democrats and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.080
the Social Democrats – spent at least&nbsp;
triple the amount of the smaller parties.&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.960
These are the key sources of funding&nbsp;
for German political parties:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:28.240
membership fees; fees paid by MPs; donations;&nbsp;
and "other" sources like sponsoring, assets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.040
shares, publications and events; and&nbsp;
then there's the big one – state funding.&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.200
This system is supposed to&nbsp;
make party financing fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.520
transparent and give parties equal&nbsp;
opportunity – and this is how it works:&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.600
The more votes a party gets,&nbsp;
the more public money it gets.&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:53.840
Public support is measured by three things:&nbsp;
votes, membership fees and donations.&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.280
For every vote a party gets, it&nbsp;
also gets €0.87 from the state.&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:08.320
For the first four million votes, the figure&nbsp;
is even a little more: €1.06 Euros per vote.&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.920
Then, for every euro a party gets in membership&nbsp;
fees, it also gets €0.45 from the state.&nbsp;

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.120
This 45-cents-per-euro rule also applies for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:23.920
personal donations - donations from&nbsp;
people, not the ones from companies.&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.480
But there are regulations: you can&nbsp;
only get these 45 cents for donations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:37.680
up to €3,300 per person, per year.
And there is a limit for the total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:46.000
amount of state financing. In 2021,&nbsp;
this limit was set at €200,049,468.&nbsp;

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:54.000
Smaller parties benefit from this system too.&nbsp;
They might have fewer supporters, but they still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.680
collect votes, membership fees and donations – and&nbsp;
according to what we've just learned, this means:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.320
They get the money from the state.
Here's an example:&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:14.320
Last year, the neo-Nazi party NPD got&nbsp;
€370,632.85 from the German state.&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.240
It's a very small amount compared to what&nbsp;
the larger parties are getting – but a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.920
of Germans aren't happy about it. Because the&nbsp;
NPD has been ruled as anti-constitutional.&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:30.720
There is a push to stop&nbsp;
public money going to the NPD.&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.720
The government has asked the&nbsp;
country's top court to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.760
state funding flowing to the extreme right.
But the court doesn't seem to be in a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.760
hurry. That was in 2019, and we're&nbsp;
still waiting for the verdict.&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.120
That's one problem in the system –&nbsp;
but critics highlight other loopholes.&nbsp;

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:57.840
There's a lack of transparency. There&nbsp;
is no limit to financial influence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.040
For example, donations to political&nbsp;
parties, there's no legal maximum.&nbsp;

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.040
Indeed: In Germany, there's no limit on&nbsp;
the amount parties can accept in donations.&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:15.600
To understand it better, let's have a look at&nbsp;
the details: There are different transparency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:24.560
rules for small, medium and large donations.
Donations under €500 can stay anonymous.&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:30.960
If donations are more than €10,000, parties&nbsp;
have to publicly declare where they came from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.360
They do this in their annual reports.
The problem here, however, is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.840
reports usually appear with a very&nbsp;
long delay - of one to two years.&nbsp;

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:47.280
Only donations over €50,000&nbsp;
have to be declared immediately.&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.160
Another loophole in the system has to do with&nbsp;
these guys here and plenty of other industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.600
players and business representatives.
Instead of making large donations, big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.000
companies often "sponsor" party events instead.
For example, a car manufacturer displaying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.320
products at a party conference – where&nbsp;
they can also network with politicians.&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.760
Sponsoring doesn't have to be declared&nbsp;
in the party's financial reports.&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:30.240
And this is really annoying. And from my&nbsp;
perspective, that sponsoring activity is still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.480
buried in this income from other&nbsp;
activities category in the annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:42.640
reports of parties. And that means it is&nbsp;
invisible, put differently: untransparent.&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.480
That brings us to another issue – the authority&nbsp;
that's supposed to check if parties are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:55.280
sticking to the rules – and fine them if not.
Critics say that body is not fit for purpose.&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.280
For example, it cannot&nbsp;
proactively investigate parties.&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:02.800
And it's also under the control&nbsp;
of the Bundestag president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:06.480
who belongs to a political party&nbsp;
himself. In the current government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.480
the president of the Bundestag is a member&nbsp;
of Angela Merkel's Christian Democrats.&nbsp;

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:18.640
So there's potential for conflict of interest.
Well, what we need is simply ameliorating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:26.400
system as it is, rendering the supervisory&nbsp;
body more independent from politics, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:34.080
recognizing that donations, at least above&nbsp;
€10,000, can really make an impact. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.120
rendering a donors identity&nbsp;
transparent immediately.&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:43.520
A look at the details makes it&nbsp;
clear: There ARE many loopholes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.560
in the system - and experts say addressing&nbsp;
these would make party financing fairer.&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:58.080
Right now what matters to parties –&nbsp;
and their bank accounts – is getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:14.720
as many votes as possible on September&nbsp;
26. Because the cents really do add up.

